   "text": {
    "L1": "Non-metals are not useless. They do important jobs in living things and in everyday life.",
    "L2": "Here is the myth: metals do all the important jobs. That is not true.",
    "L3": "Oxygen helps living things release energy from food, and it also supports burning.",
    "L4": "A healthy person gets enough oxygen from air, which contains about 21 percent oxygen. A patient with weak lungs may need oxygen from a cylinder.",
    "L5": "Carbon is part of food, our bodies and wood. Graphite in a pencil and diamond are forms of carbon.",
    "L6": "Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of air, but plants use nitrogen compounds from soil to make proteins.",
    "L7": "Nitrogen gas fills chips packets to help keep chips fresh, while chlorine kills germs in drinking water.",
    "L8": "So non-metals are essential: they support life, protect health and help us use familiar products."
   },
